  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-81506",
    "knowledge_point_id": "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Final Position of a Particle",
    "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=3.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$",
    "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{6}].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{7}{2}+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3+{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})]}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =3+([\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})]-[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0-\\frac{\\pi }{6})]) \\\\  & =3+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =3+(0-(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$",
